	<logo>https://static.globalissues.org/i/globalissues/logo-feed.jpg</logo><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44207</id><title>Strategic Blindness at UNGA 81: “Super Intelligence” and “Killer Robots” Take Center Stage as Women’s Rights Take a Back Seat</title><updated>2026-09-29T16:10:06-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44207" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/Strategic-Blindness_.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/Strategic-Blindness_.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/Strategic-Blindness_-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/Strategic-Blindness_.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
NEW YORK, September 29 (IPS)  - “Artificial intelligence (AI) must be shaped by humanity, with humanity, and for all humanity… Life-and-death decisions must never be surrendered to machines. Killer robots must have no place in our future.”
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44207&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Strategic Blindness at UNGA 81: “Super Intelligence” and “Killer Robots” Take Center Stage as Women’s Rights Take a Back Seat”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44206</id><title>Fragmented Policies Hold Back Agroecology Adoption in Africa’s Congo Basin</title><updated>2026-09-29T11:58:45-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44206" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/Mohamed-Abakar.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/Mohamed-Abakar.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/Mohamed-Abakar-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/Mohamed-Abakar.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
YAOUNDE, Cameroon, September 29 (IPS)  - Agricultural, land, forestry, environmental and trade policies in the Congo Basin remain compartmentalised at country levels, leaving proven agroecological models trapped at pilot stages due to a lack of advisory services, organic inputs, market outlets and sustainable financing.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44206&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Fragmented Policies Hold Back Agroecology Adoption in Africa’s Congo Basin”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44204</id><title>Overshoot of Paris Agreement and the Looming Super El Niño Drive Urgent Calls for Global Climate Action at UNGA 81</title><updated>2026-09-29T05:23:01-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44204" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/cs_2026.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/cs_2026.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/cs_2026-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/cs_2026.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
UNITED NATIONS, September 29 (IPS)  - On September 23, the United Nations held its 2026 Climate Summit as part of the High-Level Week of the 81st session of the General Assembly (UNGA 81). Convening civil society actors, climate scientists, major energy producers and consumers, development experts, and world leaders, the summit addressed critical strategies to mitigate the compounding impacts of the climate crisis and accelerate progress toward the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs).
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44204&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Overshoot of Paris Agreement and the Looming Super El Niño Drive Urgent Calls for Global Climate Action at UNGA 81”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44213</id><title>Funding cuts put 8.3 million refugees at risk of losing vital assistance</title><updated>2026-09-29T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44213" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/29-09-2026-UNHCR-Burundi.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/29-09-2026-UNHCR-Burundi.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/29-09-2026-UNHCR-Burundi.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/29-09-2026-UNHCR-Burundi.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Haji Mohammad Shafi spent 44 years in Pakistan before he was forced to leave. He sold everything he owned and returned to Afghanistan with his five children, arriving with almost nothing.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44213&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Funding cuts put 8.3 million refugees at risk of losing vital assistance”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44212</id><title>Clean your plate – to reduce food loss and waste</title><updated>2026-09-29T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44212" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/24-09-2026_Adobe_food-waste.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/24-09-2026_Adobe_food-waste.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/24-09-2026_Adobe_food-waste.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/24-09-2026_Adobe_food-waste.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Food waste is helping to fuel global warming, one half-eaten sandwich at a time. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44212&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Clean your plate – to reduce food loss and waste”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44211</id><title>LIVE: Weighing nuclear risks amid rising tensions and war</title><updated>2026-09-29T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44211" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/12-08-2024-IAEA-Ukraine-Zaporizhzhya-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/12-08-2024-IAEA-Ukraine-Zaporizhzhya-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/12-08-2024-IAEA-Ukraine-Zaporizhzhya-01.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/12-08-2024-IAEA-Ukraine-Zaporizhzhya-01.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Amid rising tensions, extreme weather impacts and strikes on nuclear infrastructure in war-torn Iran and Ukraine, UN Member States are returning to the core issue of disarmament on Tuesday, closing out the General Assembly’s High-Level Week with expected calls for greater safeguards.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44211&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “LIVE: Weighing nuclear risks amid rising tensions and war”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44210</id><title>Security Council LIVE: DRC peace deals fail to halt fighting as Ebola deepens crisis</title><updated>2026-09-29T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44210" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/19-08-2026_yuan8.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/19-08-2026_yuan8.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/19-08-2026_yuan8.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/19-08-2026_yuan8.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Security Council is meeting in New York on the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC) Tuesday, where clashes between Government forces and M23 rebels continue in the east despite a series of peace agreements. Ambassadors are likely to focus on ceasefire monitoring, rising tensions between the DRC and Rwanda, and the country’s worst Ebola outbreak on record.  &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44210&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Security Council LIVE: DRC peace deals fail to halt fighting as Ebola deepens crisis”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44209</id><title>DRC: Early access to care and oxygen decisive for Ebola patients’ survival – WHO</title><updated>2026-09-29T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44209" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/21-09-2026_WHO_Africa-DRC_Vaccination_ERVEBO.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/21-09-2026_WHO_Africa-DRC_Vaccination_ERVEBO.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/21-09-2026_WHO_Africa-DRC_Vaccination_ERVEBO.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/21-09-2026_WHO_Africa-DRC_Vaccination_ERVEBO.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC), health workers fighting the country’s largest-ever Ebola outbreak are struggling to overcome critical challenges to patient care, the UN World Health Organization (WHO) warned on Tuesday.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44209&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “DRC: Early access to care and oxygen decisive for Ebola patients’ survival – WHO”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44208</id><title>Yemen escalation fuels fears of further cholera, measles spread</title><updated>2026-09-29T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44208" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/25-09-2026_UNICEF_Yemen_Malnutrition.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/25-09-2026_UNICEF_Yemen_Malnutrition.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/25-09-2026_UNICEF_Yemen_Malnutrition.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/09/25-09-2026_UNICEF_Yemen_Malnutrition.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Heavy fighting in Yemen is fuelling concerns of an uptick in cholera, measles and other preventable diseases, as overcrowding at displacement sites threatens to overwhelm the limited healthcare services still open.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/29/44208&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Yemen escalation fuels fears of further cholera, measles spread”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/28/44193</id><title>India Still Needs to Address Harmful Dark Skin Stereotypes</title><updated>2026-09-28T14:40:16-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/28/44193" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/dark-skinned.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/dark-skinned.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/dark-skinned-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/09/dark-skinned.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
NEW DEHLI, September 28 (IPS)  - As a young girl, Sarada Murlidharan cringed at the thought of going to school. It was not the studies but the taunts of ‘being black’ or having dark skin, where fair skin was considered preferential, that made her unhappy.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/09/28/44193&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “India Still Needs to Address Harmful Dark Skin Stereotypes”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry></feed><!-- 0.0008s ch:1 -->